{:db/id 17592186073815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186073436 :question/id #uuid "57fc631b-c251-55d2-a9d5-6b41fc4bb00c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12612" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c48fc163b3c9543dcfdd03b20882f0d2a19747672c6b1db2a42a683f0014fe25.png)\n\n \n\nFind the point on the line $y-x=4$ that is closest to the point $(3,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-x & =4 \\\\ y & =x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y =  x + 4$ and passes through the point $(3,3).$ \n\nThe given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-1(x-3) \\\\ y-3 & =-x+3 \\\\ y & =-x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x+4 \\\\ y=-x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =-x+6 \\\\ x+x & =6-4 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=5.$ So, our solution is $x=1$ and $y=5.$ Consequently, the lines intersect at the point $(1,5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y-x= 4$ that is closest to the point $(3,3)$ is  $(1,5).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049869 :topic/math-academy-id 408 :topic/title "Calculating the Intersection of Two Lines"} {:db/id 17592186050473 :topic/math-academy-id 3562 :topic/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Lines"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186074513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0498568d-b327-5f78-89e8-8587c0368b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id 17592186339162} {:answer/id #uuid "850104ea-f24f-5eb3-bc01-1f1701a3ca6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id 17592186340323} {:answer/id #uuid "a884e107-b09a-542c-94ff-f2323802311a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id 17592186340660} {:answer/id #uuid "c8516292-870e-5d6a-9fc2-73422fa2795d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{4})" :db/id 17592186340902} {:answer/id #uuid "d93f1851-2a54-53fb-9fee-5fb53f3f5e43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id 17592186341056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a884e107-b09a-542c-94ff-f2323802311a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id 17592186340660} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1068806-46ea-595d-a611-e82e67e43211" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341781}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2a5b92a-6d2c-561b-999b-9dedaf72311f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74060" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f97972b1765e58a76475a99a97e4a8818b05efa1f26a79a232b250b6ef681e9b.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $2y-3x=3$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3x & =3 \\\\ 2y & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}$ and passes through the point $(2,-2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-2) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{6}{13}\\cdot \\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=0.$ So, our solution is $x=-1$ and $y=0.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-1,0).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $2y-3x=3$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2)$ is  $(-1,0).$"} {:db/id 17592186074514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6304ff82-aa6c-50b7-b79e-a11e972b7aa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id 17592186340009} {:answer/id #uuid "7d839962-7657-5bb7-904b-97d127c09ca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{9}{2},1)" :db/id 17592186340259} {:answer/id #uuid "a38a60c9-c99e-5333-a142-a09672caa6e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},-1)" :db/id 17592186340611} {:answer/id #uuid "befaa2f8-684a-5c95-bb90-4e92f8bb8e1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id 17592186340830} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc6bc70-eb00-5071-8312-8709fa64cf86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-4)" :db/id 17592186341090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6304ff82-aa6c-50b7-b79e-a11e972b7aa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id 17592186340009} :answer-field/id #uuid "f952c9dc-1256-5a8a-9688-3f9bfd070213" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341826}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "853cfa3c-da08-562e-b7e9-24b69cddbfcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-74061" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d478fb4499ee19a7c385ae93dd5ad790404351ceda63c20341b9996a1466ca60.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ that is closest to the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ and passes through the point $(2,1).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{3}{2}x-2 \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x & =-\\frac{2}{3}x \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-2.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=-2.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(0,-2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ that is closest to the point $(2,1)$ is $(0,-2).$"} {:db/id 17592186250245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "321312ca-35e6-590b-b440-c12b3be53dcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{66}{13},-\\frac{18}{13})" :db/id 17592186247881} {:answer/id #uuid "73f9e2ae-8b3b-58ab-9adc-90853b9cafce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{90}{13})" :db/id 17592186248444} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcc3641-ada3-55ad-bd99-688a6222a88b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13})" :db/id 17592186248510} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb2bdd5-d7c7-5d30-bb92-8a47f30a62f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{18}{13},-\\frac{66}{13})" :db/id 17592186248809} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f270dd-8b9b-593e-b900-f2f6c240780a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13})" :db/id 17592186249594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8f270dd-8b9b-593e-b900-f2f6c240780a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13})" :db/id 17592186249594} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d014b53-8f67-50f0-a715-6786d9eb85c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186249691}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e7059ea-e4f5-5468-9ed3-af001f239b6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-114336" :question/problem "![](images/69/69e06e1b9b72e3a4c1fc2e7f2fcd85aa4aa8b34e4fa2f4f5aebb8d1b10474415.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ that is closest to the point $(-3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ and passes through the point $(-3,4).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x+3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+2+4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x+6 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =6-3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{18}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{18}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{66}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=-\\frac{18}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{66}{13}.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ that is closest to the point $(-3,4)$ is $(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13}).$"} {:db/id 17592186250248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072e45bc-d35a-54a2-8ab9-5f295ebf110f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id 17592186247490} {:answer/id #uuid "14581a73-ac53-505c-8447-0394a875fcd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},-\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id 17592186247608} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb5a940-7e41-5de0-9f76-33adbc3d81da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id 17592186248386} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6d5bb5-c38f-58f9-bdcf-296b1625b1d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{11}{13},\\frac{3}{13})" :db/id 17592186249109} {:answer/id #uuid "de549bfd-d213-57c1-ad88-fadc596d833b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,11)" :db/id 17592186249395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "072e45bc-d35a-54a2-8ab9-5f295ebf110f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id 17592186247490} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5d3e168-c279-50d0-a119-0930eb63b6db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186249960}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd8e8e07-0b88-58f7-806a-13412093afb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-114345" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff9810290868f7fd465bc310c969255b92f646d6b59ad71a93a3dcc9c38ef49c.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+2x & =3 \\\\ 3y & =-2x+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1$ and passes through the point $(3,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}+5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{3}{2}x & =\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5)$ is  $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$"} {:db/id 17592186250249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bf8bb55-4020-5f0b-bd59-42ee32fbb771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{5},\\frac{1}{5})" :db/id 17592186247677} {:answer/id #uuid "506a9fe3-70ee-5719-9e0a-dcce648d0473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5})" :db/id 17592186248144} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf2ce60-0e78-5b08-b8b7-0a60d6e30d7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5})" :db/id 17592186248821} {:answer/id #uuid "bb02feef-1bc2-517b-96ff-29402a331b44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,7)" :db/id 17592186249094} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb3d233-8364-50ab-b259-e0a0966e7b9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{5},-\\frac{7}{5})" :db/id 17592186249364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cf2ce60-0e78-5b08-b8b7-0a60d6e30d7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5})" :db/id 17592186248821} :answer-field/id #uuid "2932adcc-4eb8-504c-836b-faa4fbe63c1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186249750}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3954acb1-fda8-5e85-a442-60c3272bf340" :question/math-academy-id "q-114346" :question/problem "![](images/df/df3838ae44d1477a4a0de303972cd26a2ceaad9b213f1d8e4be3a53bd453aeb2.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y-3x=2$ that is closest to the point $(4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3x & =2 \\\\ y & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=3x+2$ and passes through the point $(4,0).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $3,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-4) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=3x+2 \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 3x+\\frac{1}{3}x & =\\frac{4}{3}-2 \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}x & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 10x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{5}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{7}{5}.$ So, our solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $y=\\frac{7}{5}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $y-3x=2$ that is closest to the point $(4,0)$ is  $(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5}).$"} {:db/id 17592186250250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ce54910-1244-5386-86d8-f09d91856276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id 17592186247960} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6a67d4-b82b-5092-bade-43eff2cd1bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id 17592186248536} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e5e077-c051-52e8-9324-38476f689c97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id 17592186249076} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ca5e2c-1d09-5ba6-bac7-e091b9182a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{9}{5},\\frac{2}{5})" :db/id 17592186249285} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e2987c-b486-55c8-9dc4-132ab31c0731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id 17592186249450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e6a67d4-b82b-5092-bade-43eff2cd1bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id 17592186248536} :answer-field/id #uuid "c816ccdc-152d-585a-8fbf-41891b81689b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186250013}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "855df716-3231-50d2-97cf-6767626cecd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-114349" :question/problem "![](images/ba/ba46b4f3f458a6be13ea01ca1351658c102732db94e22725890afaaade6fc904.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $5y-10x=5$ that is closest to the point $(-2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-10x & =5 \\\\ 5y & =10x+5 \\\\ y & =2x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=2x+1$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $2,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2x+1 \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{1}{2}x & =2-1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{9}{5}.$ So, our solution is $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ and $y=\\frac{9}{5}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $5y-10x=5$ that is closest to the point $(-2,3)$ is  $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5}).$"} {:db/id 17592186342210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "897863c7-0053-5881-8309-0bafe6422e75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-5)" :db/id 17592186340355} {:answer/id #uuid "a9eb5854-49b2-5f3d-8f1a-f3ede7741417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{15}{2})" :db/id 17592186340676} {:answer/id #uuid "b538ac87-ef4c-5ab5-bea9-11f7323e59a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-2)" :db/id 17592186340772} {:answer/id #uuid "c7070e71-630e-52cf-86b9-7b5b1c88b417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-\\frac{13}{2})" :db/id 17592186340891} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c949a5-d093-5c67-b8fe-bda979b82d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{29}{4})" :db/id 17592186341054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "897863c7-0053-5881-8309-0bafe6422e75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-5)" :db/id 17592186340355} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ed75246-b3cc-5428-993e-e7cf98157952" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341493}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7a18e4a-49f2-563e-8c4d-e70b3f9189cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-74421" :question/problem "![](images/67/67d09cf1ebb51a37c122ea1e0bb53b0bcabedca0251ae4105ebb93dd55d111dd.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8$ that is closest to the point $(-4,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8$ and passes through the point $(-4,-1).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x+4) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8 \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x-8 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =8-\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{13}{3}\\cdot \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-5.$ So, our solution is $x=2$ and $y=-5.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(2,-5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8$ that is closest to the point $(-4,-1)$ is $(2,-5).$"} {:db/id 17592186342212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47f7ce0c-16e5-531a-94b9-547622df3f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id 17592186339767} {:answer/id #uuid "569cbb0a-f99c-5355-8f53-bdc678082993" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/id 17592186339923} {:answer/id #uuid "8688ee8a-8219-5c84-a042-632559c1a6df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id 17592186340337} {:answer/id #uuid "95f977c5-80d6-5f10-bfe2-b1560cf968b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-1)" :db/id 17592186340481} {:answer/id #uuid "ae765e2d-062d-5b17-8c52-1eef7d4ff32e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,1)" :db/id 17592186340720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95f977c5-80d6-5f10-bfe2-b1560cf968b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-1)" :db/id 17592186340481} :answer-field/id #uuid "73585de0-1b70-548d-9d76-12f3a61d673b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341582}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7220b7e3-9cce-5102-874c-014511b9e7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-74439" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d687bcdc23c283e0945b039781450d2a020f0fd89c0d2976bb252914b296978.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+x & =-4 \\\\ y & =-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-x-4$ and passes through the point $(0,2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-2 & =x \\\\ y & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=x+2 \\\\ y=-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =-x-4 \\\\ x+x & =-4-2 \\\\ 2x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-1.$ So, our solution is $x=-3$ and $y=-1.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-3,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2)$ is  $(-3,-1).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}
